IronRDP web client — vendored bundle
====================================

Source packages (npm), pinned:

  @devolutions/iron-remote-desktop      0.11.0
  @devolutions/iron-remote-desktop-rdp  0.7.0

Upstream:  https://github.com/Devolutions/IronRDP
Licence:   MIT OR Apache-2.0  (see LICENSE-MIT next to this file)
Vendored:  2026-08-14

The bundle is NOT a verbatim copy: the WebAssembly module has been lifted out
of the JavaScript. See "The one patch" below — it is not cosmetic, the plugin
does not work without it.

Why vendored rather than fetched at build time
----------------------------------------------

The bundle ships inside the signed `.xqsp` and is served to the browser by the
host's embed broker. A build-time download would put a network fetch between
"what we reviewed" and "what the user runs"; keeping the bytes in the
repository means the signature covers exactly what was reviewed.

The `.d.ts` files are vendored alongside the code deliberately. They are not
needed at runtime, but they are the only documentation of this API that is
guaranteed to match the pinned version, and the 0.x series changes shape
between minor releases.

The one patch
-------------

Upstream ships the WebAssembly module inlined into the JavaScript as a
`data:application/wasm;base64,…` string, which its loader retrieves with
`fetch()`. The host serves embed routes with `connect-src 'self'`, and `data:`
is not part of that: the fetch is blocked, `init()` fails with "Failed to
fetch", and the page never even opens its tunnel. Observed live, not deduced.

So `cmd/xqs-rdp-vendor` decodes that base64 into `iron-remote-desktop-rdp.wasm`
next to the module and replaces the string literal with

    new URL("./iron-remote-desktop-rdp.wasm", import.meta.url)

The request becomes same-origin and passes. The loader accepts a URL exactly
where it accepted a string, so nothing else changes, and Go serves `.wasm` as
`application/wasm`, so the fast `instantiateStreaming` path still applies.

Side effect: 6.1 MB of JavaScript becomes 75 KB of JavaScript plus 4.3 MB of
WebAssembly — base64 costs a third more than the bytes it encodes.

Verifying and updating
----------------------

    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
    go run ./cmd/xqs-rdp-vendor        # re-does the copy and the patch

Run the vendor command rather than copying by hand: the patch has to be applied
identically every time, and "remember to also extract the wasm" is exactly the
kind of step that gets skipped once.

Read the upstream changelog first. This is a 0.x package, and a breaking change
between minor versions is normal rather than exceptional. Two things must keep
holding:

  - `enableCredssp(false)` must still exist and still work. The plugin's entire
    security model rests on the browser not performing NLA.
  - the wasm must still be inlined as a data URI, or the patch must be revisited.
    The vendor command fails loudly if it cannot find one, rather than quietly
    producing a bundle that would be blocked at runtime.
